"Web Animations Module Level 1 (https://drafts.csswg.org/web-animations-1/)\nciteturn2368view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2361view0\",\"lineno\":1758}); Total lines: 4503\nL1753: Each 【379†keyframe】 may have a keyframe-specific composite operation that, if set, is applied to all values specified in that keyframe. The possible operations and their meanings are identical to those defined for the 【381†composite operation】 associated with the 【237†keyframe effect】 as a whole in 【108†§ 5.4.4 Effect composition】.\nL1754: If the 【382†keyframe-specific composite operation】 for a keyframe is not set, the composite operation specified for the keyframe effect as a whole is used for values specified in that keyframe.\nL1755: Tests\nL1756: \nL1757:   * 【383†applying-interpolated-transform.html†wpt.fyi】 【384†(live test)†wpt.live】 【385†(source)†github.com】\nL1758: #### 5.3.2. Computing property values\nL1759: \nL1760: To compute a property value given a property `property`, a value `value`, and an `【373†Element†dom.spec.whatwg.org】` `element`: resolve `value` according to the “Computed Value” line of the `property`’s definition table, using the 【362†computed values】 of `element` as the context for resolving dependencies, and return the result.\nL1761: \nL1762: Note: The 【362†computed values】 on `element` are not affected by this algorithm.\nL1763: This algorithm implies that property values specified in keyframes can establish order dependencies. When 【386†computing a property value】, the 【362†computed values】 of dependencies held by `value` must be calculated first.\nL1764: \nL1765:     var animation = elem.animate([{ fontSize: '10px', width: '10em' },\nL1766:                                   { fontSize: '20px', width: '20em' }], 1000);\nL1767:     animation.currentTime = 500;\nL1768:     console.log(getComputedStyle(elem).fontSize); // Should be 15px\nL1769:     console.log(getComputedStyle(elem).width); // Should be 225px\nL1770: In this example, in order to 【386†compute a property value】 for `10em`, we need to know the 【362†computed value】 of 【387†font-size】 on the 【374†target element】, which in turn is determined by the 【378†effect value】 for font-size, which in turn depends on computing property values for font-size. Hence, computing property values are subject to ordering constraints.\nL1771: #### 5.3.3. Calculating computed keyframes\nL1772: \nL1773: Before calculating the 【378†effect value】 of a 【237†keyframe effect】, the property values on its 【379†keyframes】 are 【101†computed】, and the offset to use for any keyframes with a null 【380†keyframe offset】 is computed. The result of resolving these values is a set of computed keyframes.\nL1774: \nL1775: The calculated 【380†keyframe offsets】 of a set of 【379†keyframe】 that includes suitable values for each null keyframe offset are referred to as the computed keyframe offsets.\nL1776: To produce 【388†computed keyframe offsets】, we define a procedure to compute missing keyframe offsets that takes a sequence of 【379†keyframes】, `keyframes`, and has the following steps:\nL1777: \nL1778:   1. For each 【379†keyframe】 in `keyframes`, let the 【388†computed keyframe offset】 of the keyframe be equal to its 【380†keyframe offset】 value.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWeb Animations Module Level 1 (https://drafts.csswg.org/web-animations-1/)\nciteturn2368view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2361view0\",\"lineno\":1804}); Total lines: 4503\nL1795:     3. Let `index` refer to the position of `keyframe` in the sequence of keyframes between `A` and `B` such that the first keyframe after `A` has an `index` of 1.\nL1796: \nL1797:     4. Set the 【388†computed keyframe offset】 of `keyframe` to 【389†offset】_{`A`} + (offset_{`B`} − offset_{`A`}) × `index` / `n`.\nL1798: \nL1799: Tests\nL1800:   * 【390†background-shorthand.html†wpt.fyi】 【391†(live test)†wpt.live】 【392†(source)†github.com】\nL1801:   * 【393†computed-keyframes-shorthands.html†wpt.fyi】 【394†(live test)†wpt.live】 【395†(source)†github.com】\nL1802:   * 【396†effect-value-context-filling.html†wpt.fyi】 【397†(live test)†wpt.live】 【398†(source)†github.com】\nL1803:   * 【399†effect-value-context.html†wpt.fyi】 【400†(live test)†wpt.live】 【401†(source)†github.com】\nL1804: 【402†Computed keyframes】 are produced using the following procedure. Note that this procedure is only performed on a 【237†keyframe effect】 having an 【312†effect target】 for which computed property values can be calculated.\nL1805: \nL1806:   1. Let `computed keyframes` be an empty list of 【379†keyframes】.\nL1807: \nL1808:   2. For each `keyframe` in the list of 【379†keyframes】 specified on this 【237†keyframe effect】, perform the following steps:\nL1809: \nL1810:     1. Add a new empty 【379†keyframe】, `computed keyframe`, to `computed keyframes`.\nL1811:     2. For each property specified in `keyframe`:\nL1812: \nL1813:        * 【386†Compute a property value】 using the value specified on `keyframe` as the value, and the 【374†target element】 as the element; then add the property and resulting value to `computed keyframe`.\nL1814: \nL1815:        * For shorthand properties, add the equivalent longhand properties.\nL1816:        * For logical properties 【403†[CSS-LOGICAL-1]】, add the 【404†equivalent physical properties】 【405†[CSS-WRITING-MODES-4]】 based on the computed value of 【406†writing-mode】 and/or 【407†direction】 for the 【312†effect target】.\nL1817: For example, if `keyframe` has a value of 12pt for the 【408†border-width】 【409†shorthand property】, the user agent will 【386†compute a property value】 of 16px for each of the 【410†longhand properties】: 【411†border-bottom-width】, 【412†border-left-width】, 【413†border-right-width】, and 【414†border-top-width】. As a result, `computed keyframe` would not have a value for the border-width property, but would instead include each of the longhand properties, each with the value 16px.\nL1818: If conflicts arise when expanding shorthand properties or replacing logical properties with physical properties, apply the following rules in order until the conflict is resolved:\nL1819: \nL1820:       1. Longhand properties override shorthand properties (e.g. 【415†border-top-color】 overrides 【416†border-top】).\nL1821: \nL1822:       2. Shorthand properties with fewer longhand components override those with more longhand components (e.g. 【416†border-top】 overrides 【417†border-color】).\nL1823:       3. Physical properties override logical properties.\nL1824: \nL1825:       4. For shorthand properties with an equal number of longhand components, properties whose IDL name (see the 【418†CSS property to IDL attribute】 algorithm 【419†[CSSOM]】) appears earlier when sorted in ascending order by the Unicode codepoints that make up each IDL name, override those who appear later.\nL1826: \nL1827:   3. Apply the procedure to 【420†compute missing keyframe offsets】 to `computed keyframes`.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWeb Animations Module Level 1 (https://drafts.csswg.org/web-animations-1/)\nciteturn2368view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2361view0\",\"lineno\":1941}); Total lines: 4503\nL1930: For example, animations whose 【226†class】 is “CSS animation” are defined as having a higher composite order than animations whose class is “CSS transition” but lower than other animations without a specific class.\nL1931: \nL1932: Within the set of “CSS animation” objects, specialized composite ordering is defined based on the 【446†animation-name】 property amongst other factors.\nL1933: #### 5.4.2. The effect stack\nL1934: \nL1935: An effect stack is associated with each property 【186†targeted】 by one or more 【237†keyframe effects】. The 【355†effect stack】 establishes the relative composite order of keyframe effects.\nL1936: \nL1937: The relative composite order of any two 【237†keyframe effects】 `A` and `B` within an 【355†effect stack】 is established by comparing their properties as follows:\nL1938: \nL1939:   1. Sort `A` and `B` by applying the following conditions in turn until the order is resolved,\nL1940:     1. If `A` and `B`’s 【288†associated animations】 differ by 【226†class】, sort by any inter-class composite order as defined for the corresponding classes.\nL1941: \nL1942:     2. If `A` and `B` are still not sorted, sort by any 【226†class】-specific composite order defined by the common class of `A` and `B`’s 【288†associated animations】.\nL1943: \nL1944:     3. If `A` and `B` are still not sorted, sort by the position of their 【288†associated animations】 in the 【447†global animation list】.\nL1945: 【174†Animation effects】 that sort earlier have lower composite order.\nL1946: #### 5.4.3. Calculating the result of an effect stack\nL1947: \nL1948: In order to calculate the final value of an 【355†effect stack】, the 【378†effect values】 of each 【237†keyframe effect】 in the stack are combined in composite order.\nL1949: \nL1950: Each step in the process of evaluating an 【355†effect stack】 takes an underlying value as input.\nL1951: For each 【237†keyframe effect】 in the stack, the appropriate 【378†effect value】 from the keyframe effect is combined with the 【356†underlying value】 to produce a new value. This resulting value becomes the underlying value for combining the next keyframe effect in the stack.\nL1952: The final value of an 【355†effect stack】, called the composited value, is simply the result of combining the 【378†effect value】 of the final (highest composite order) 【237†keyframe effect】 in the stack with the 【356†underlying value】 at that point.\nL1953: #### 5.4.4. Effect composition\nL1954: \nL1955: The specific operation used to combine an 【378†effect value】 with an【356†underlying value】 is determined by the composite operation of the 【237†keyframe effect】 that produced the effect value.\nL1956: \nL1957: This specification defines three 【381†composite operations】 as follows:\nL1958: \nL1959: replace\nL1960: \nL1961: \nL1962: The result of compositing the 【378†effect value】 with the 【356†underlying value】 is simply the effect value.\nL1963: \nL1964: add\nL1965: The 【378†effect value】 is 【448†added】 to the 【356†underlying value】. For 【358†animation types】 where the addition operation is defined such that it is not commutative, the order of the operands is `【356†underlying value】 + 【378†effect value】`.\nL1966: \nL1967: accumulate\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCSS Values and Units Module Level 5 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-values-5/)\nciteturn2368view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2361view1\",\"lineno\":1602}); Total lines: 2555\nL1600: A function’s 【525†replacement】 algorithm is called with the results of 【78†early substitution】 and parsing with the function’s 【521†argument grammar】, but nothing more. The algorithm will have to further 【171†substitute】 each argument it deals with, before 【160†parsing】 it as the appropriate part of its normal grammar, and then performing whatever logic it needs.\nL1601: It might leave some arguments completely unsubstituted (for example, the fallback argument of an 【138†attr()】 function, if the attribute value exists and correctly parses), or only parse some arguments based on the results of evaluating other arguments (for example, later 【515†<if-condition>】 values in an 【115†if()】 function are only evaluated if earlier ones evaluated as false, and only the result in the \"successful\" 【514†<if-branch>】 is evaluated).\nL1602: To substitute arbitrary substitution functions in a sequence of 【168†component values】 `values`, given an optional 【529†substitution context】 `context`:\nL1603: \nL1604:   1. 【528†Guard】 `context` for the remainder of this algorithm. If `context` is marked as a 【530†cyclic substitution context】, return the 【169†guaranteed-invalid value】.\nL1605:   2. 【526†For each†infra.spec.whatwg.org】 【126†arbitrary substitution function】 `func` in `values` (ordered via a depth-first pre-order traversal) that is not nested in the contents of another arbitrary substitution function:\nL1606: \nL1607:     1. 【863†Substitute early-invoked functions】 in `func`’s contents, and let `early result` be the result.\nL1608: \nL1609:     2. If `early result` contains the 【169†guaranteed-invalid value】, replace `func` in `values` with the guaranteed-invalid value and 【527†continue†infra.spec.whatwg.org】.\nL1610:     3. 【160†Parse】 `early result` according to `func`’s 【521†argument grammar】. If this returns failure, replace `func` in values with the 【169†guaranteed-invalid value】 and 【527†continue†infra.spec.whatwg.org】; otherwise, let `arguments` be the result.\nL1611: \nL1612:     4. 【525†Replace an arbitrary substitution function】 for `func`, given `arguments`, as defined by that function. Let `result` be the returned list of 【168†component values】.\nL1613:     5. If `result` contains the 【169†guaranteed-invalid value】, replace `func` in `values` with the guaranteed-invalid value. Otherwise, replace `func` in `values` with `result`.\nL1614: \nL1615:   3. If `context` is marked as a 【530†cyclic substitution context】, return the 【169†guaranteed-invalid value】. Nested 【126†arbitrary substitution functions】 may have marked `context` as 【530†cyclic】 in step 2.\nL1616: \nL1617:   4. Return `values`.\nL1618: A substitution context is a 【154†list†infra.spec.whatwg.org】 consisting of: the dependency type, as a string; one or more additional values, specific to the dependency type. (Usually, just one additional string.)\nL1619: \nL1620: As 【126†arbitrary substitution functions】 are 【864†resolved†webidl.spec.whatwg.org】 they accumulate 【529†substitution contexts】 which affect how nested arbitrary substitution functions resolve.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCSS Values and Units Module Level 5 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-values-5/)\nciteturn2368view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2361view1\",\"lineno\":1773}); Total lines: 2555\nL1759: For example, the following usage is fine from a syntax standpoint, but results in nonsense when the variable is substituted in:\nL1760: \nL1761:     :root { --looks-valid: 20px; }\nL1762:     p { background-color: var(--looks-valid); }\nL1763: \nL1764: \nL1765: Since 20px is an invalid value for 【916†background-color】, the property becomes 【486†invalid at computed-value time】, and instead resolves to 【917†transparent】 (the 【918†initial value】 for background-color).\nL1766: If the property was one that’s inherited by default, such as 【919†color】, it would compute to the inherited value rather than the initial value.\nL1767: \nL1768: While a 【127†var()】 function can’t get a 【484†CSS-wide keyword】 from the 【542†custom property】 itself—​if you tried to specify that, like --foo: initial;, it would just trigger 【920†explicit defaulting】 for the custom property—​it can have a CSS-wide keyword in its fallback:\nL1769: \nL1770:     p { color: var(--does-not-exist, initial); }\nL1771: In the above code, if the --does-not-exist property didn’t exist or is 【486†invalid at computed-value time】, the 【127†var()】 will instead substitute in the 【921†initial】 keyword, making the property behave as if it was originally 【919†color: initial】. This will make it take on the document’s initial color value, rather than defaulting to inheritance, as it would if there were no fallback.\nL1772: \nL1773: To replace substitution functions in a property `prop`:\nL1774:   1. 【171†Substitute arbitrary substitution functions】 in `prop`’s value, given «\"property\", `prop`’s name» as the 【529†substitution context】. Let `result` be the returned 【168†component value】 sequence.\nL1775: \nL1776:   2. If `result` contains the 【169†guaranteed-invalid value】, `prop` is 【486†invalid at computed-value time】; return.\nL1777: \nL1778:   3. 【160†Parse】 `result` according to `prop`’s grammar. If this returns failure, `prop` is 【486†invalid at computed-value time】; return.\nL1779:   4. Otherwise, replace `prop`’s value with the parsed result.\nL1780: \nL1781: Tests\nL1782:   * 【922†css-variable-change-style-001.html†wpt.fyi】 【923†(live test)†wpt.live】 【924†(source)†github.com】\nL1783:   * 【925†css-variable-change-style-002.html†wpt.fyi】 【926†(live test)†wpt.live】 【927†(source)†github.com】\nL1784:   * 【928†variable-declaration-01.html†wpt.fyi】 【929†(live test)†wpt.live】 【930†(source)†github.com】\nL1785:   * 【931†variable-declaration-02.html†wpt.fyi】 【932†(live test)†wpt.live】 【933†(source)†github.com】\nL1786:   * 【934†variable-declaration-03.html†wpt.fyi】 【935†(live test)†wpt.live】 【936†(source)†github.com】\nL1787:   * 【937†variable-declaration-04.html†wpt.fyi】 【938†(live test)†wpt.live】 【939†(source)†github.com】\nL1788:   * 【940†variable-declaration-05.html†wpt.fyi】 【941†(live test)†wpt.live】 【942†(source)†github.com】\nL1789:   * 【943†variable-generated-content-dynamic-001.html†wpt.fyi】 【944†(live test)†wpt.live】 【945†(source)†github.com】\nL1790:   * 【946†variable-presentation-attribute.html†wpt.fyi】 【947†(live test)†wpt.live】 【948†(source)†github.com】\nL1791:   * 【949†variable-reference-01.html†wpt.fyi】 【950†(live test)†wpt.live】 【951†(source)†github.com】\nL1792:   * 【952†variable-reference-02.html†wpt.fyi】 【953†(live test)†wpt.live】 【954†(source)†github.com】\nL1793:   * 【955†variable-reference-03.html†wpt.fyi】 【956†(live test)†wpt.live】 【957†(source)†github.com】\nL1794:   * 【958†variable-reference-04.html†wpt.fyi】 【959†(live test)†wpt.live】 【960†(source)†github.com】\nL1795:   * 【961†variable-reference-05.html†wpt.fyi】 【962†(live test)†wpt.live】 【963†(source)†github.com】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCSS Custom Properties for Cascading Variables Module Level 1 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-variables-1/)\nciteturn2368view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2361view2\",\"lineno\":408}); Total lines: 786\nL390: The fallback value allows for some types of defensive coding. For example, an author may create a component intended to be included in a larger application, and use variables to style it so that it’s easy for the author of the larger application to theme the component to match the rest of the app.\nL391: Without fallback, the app author must supply a value for every variable that your component uses. With fallback, the component author can supply defaults, so the app author only needs to supply values for the variables they wish to override.\nL392: \nL393:     /* In the component's style: */\nL394:     .component .header {\nL395:       color: var(--header-color, blue);\nL396:     }\nL397:     .component .text {\nL398:       color: var(--text-color, black);\nL399:     }\nL400: \nL401:     /* In the larger application's style: */\nL402:     .component {\nL403:       --text-color: #080;\nL404:       /* header-color isn't set,\nL405:          and so remains blue,\nL406:          the fallback value */\nL407:     }\nL408: To 【577†replace a var() function】, given a list of `arguments`:\nL409: \nL410:   1. Let `el` be the element that the style containing the 【55†var()】 function is being applied to. Let `first arg` be the first 【63†<declaration-value>】 in `arguments`. Let `second arg` be the <declaration-value>? passed after the comma, or null if there was no comma.\nL411:   2. 【61†Substitute arbitrary substitution functions】 in `first arg`, then 【578†parse】 it as a 【276†<custom-property-name>】. If parsing returned a <custom-property-name>, let `result` be the 【271†computed value】 of the corresponding 【54†custom property】 on `el`. Otherwise, let `result` be the 【66†guaranteed-invalid value】.\nL412: \nL413: Note: Determining the 【271†computed value】 for the 【54†custom property】 implies that 【579†property replacement】 takes place, which may cause a 【580†cycle】.\nL414:   3. If the 【54†custom property】 named by the 【55†var()】’s first argument is 【227†animation-tainted】, and the var() is being used in a property that is 【581†not animatable】, set `result` to the 【66†guaranteed-invalid value】.\nL415: \nL416:   4. If `result` contains the 【66†guaranteed-invalid value】, and `second arg` was provided, set `result` to the result of 【61†substitute arbitrary substitution functions】 on `second arg`.\nL417: \nL418:   5. Return `result`.\nL419: Due to 【579†property replacement】, 【54†custom properties】 can form 【580†cycles】:\nL420: \nL421:     :root {\nL422:       --one: calc(var(--two) + 20px);\nL423:       --two: calc(var(--one) - 20px);\nL424:     }\nL425: \nL426: \nL427: In the above, both --one and --two compute to the 【66†guaranteed-invalid value】, since their 【582†substitution contexts】 have been marked as cyclic.\nL428: \nL429: Tests\nL430:   * 【583†variable-cycles.html†wpt.fyi】 【584†(live test)†wpt.live】 【585†(source)†github.com】\nL431:   * 【586†variable-declaration-30.html†wpt.fyi】 【587†(live test)†wpt.live】 【588†(source)†github.com】\nL432:   * 【589†variable-declaration-48.html†wpt.fyi】 【590†(live test)†wpt.live】 【591†(source)†github.com】\nL433:   * 【592†variable-declaration-49.html†wpt.fyi】 【593†(live test)†wpt.live】 【594†(source)†github.com】\nL434:   * 【595†variable-declaration-50.html†wpt.fyi】 【596†(live test)†wpt.live】 【597†(source)†github.com】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCSS Properties and Values API Level 1 (https://drafts.css-houdini.org/css-properties-values-api-1/)\nciteturn2368view6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2361view3\",\"lineno\":140}); Total lines: 1339\nL136: Throwing away syntax-violating custom properties would thus only be, at best, a convenience for the page author, not a necessity like for UA-defined properties.\nL137: ### 2.3. 【90†Specified Value†drafts.csswg.org】-Time Behavior\nL138: \nL139: Just like unregistered 【77†custom properties†drafts.csswg.org】, all 【81†registered custom properties】, regardless of registered syntax, accept the 【91†CSS-wide keywords†drafts.csswg.org】, such as 【92†inherit†drafts.csswg.org】 or 【93†revert†drafts.csswg.org】. Their behavior is defined in 【94†CSS Cascading 4 § 7.3 Explicit Defaulting†drafts.csswg.org】.\nL140: ### 2.4. 【95†Computed Value†drafts.csswg.org】-Time Behavior\nL141: \nL142: The 【95†computed value†drafts.csswg.org】 of a 【81†registered custom property】 is determined by the syntax of its 【96†registration】.\nL143: \nL144: If the 【96†registration’s】 syntax is the 【97†universal syntax definition】, the 【95†computed value†drafts.csswg.org】 is the same as for unregistered 【77†custom properties†drafts.csswg.org】 (either the specified value with variables substituted, or the 【87†guaranteed-invalid value†drafts.csswg.org】).\nL145: Otherwise, attempt to 【86†parse†drafts.csswg.org】 the property’s value according to its registered syntax. If this fails, the declaration is 【98†invalid at computed-value time†drafts.csswg.org】 and the 【95†computed value†drafts.csswg.org】 is determined accordingly. If it succeeds, the 【95†computed value†drafts.csswg.org】 depends on the specifics of the syntax:\nL146: \nL147: For `\"<length>\"`, `\"<length-percentage>\"`, `\"<angle>\"`, `\"<time>\"`, `\"<resolution>\"`, `\"<integer>\"`, `\"<number>\"`, and `\"<percentage>\"` values:\nL148:   * If the specified value is a 【99†dimension†drafts.csswg.org】 literal (such as 50em or .2s), the computed value is the same value, but with the unit converted to the corresponding 【100†canonical unit†drafts.csswg.org】 for the type of value.\nL149: \nL150:   * If the specified value is any other numeric literal (such as 5 or 20%), the computed value is as specified. (In particular, percentages are never resolved against anything.)\nL151:   * If the specified value is a function that evaluates to one of those types (such as a 【101†math function†drafts.csswg.org】), the computed value is defined by that function.\nL152: \nL153: For `\"<string>\"` values, the computed value is as specified.\nL154: \nL155: For `\"<color>\"` values, the value is computed by resolving color values, per 【102†CSS Color 4 §  15. Resolving <color> Values†drafts.csswg.org】.\nL156: \nL157: For `\"<custom-ident>\"`, ident, or `\"*\"` values, the computed value is as specified.\nL158: For `\"<url>\"` values, the computed value is one of the following:\nL159: \nL160:   * if the URL is a relative URL, the computed value is the resolved absolute URL as described in 【103†[css3-values]】.\nL161: \nL162:   * otherwise, the computed value is as specified.\nL163: \nL164: URL behavior examples\nL165: \nL166: Because URLs resolve against the base URL of the stylesheet they appear in, we can end up with multiple relative URLs that resolve against different base URLs, even though they appear in the same property.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[CSSWG] Minutes Lisbon F2F 2016-09-19 Part V: Transforms, Variables, Transitions/Animations, Cascade, CR Plan, January Meeting [css-transforms] [css-variables] [css-transitions] [css-animations] [css-cascade] from Dael Jackson on 2016-11-16 (www-style@w3.org from November 2016) (https://lists.w3.org/Archives/Public/www-style/2016Nov/0073.html)\nciteturn2368view7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2367view0\",\"lineno\":280}); Total lines: 725\nL258:       dbaron: If there's a dynamic change, need to go back to the\nL259:               original one...\nL260:       dbaron: Or keep 2 sets of selectors for each element\nL261:       shane: You need to do that anyway.\nL262:       TabAtkins: Not generally, but if you are doing var in keyframes,\nL263:                  which apparently you do.\nL264:       TabAtkins: Then you do.\nL265: \nL266:       dbaron: I'm not sure we did it intentionally.\nL267:       TabAtkins: var() in keyframes takes the value from the element the\nL268:                  animation is running on.\nL269:       TabAtkins: To do that properly, need to do that once, then\nL270:                  resolve, then resubstitute.\nL271:       TabAtkins: Or just do it once and animate a property that doesn't\nL272:                  get reflected, very weird.\nL273:       dbaron: I feel like you're crossing var() in keyframes and\nL274:               --custom-prop in keyframes.\nL275:       dbaron: Problem you described is problem with having custom-prop\nL276:               in keyframes, not having var()\nL277:       TabAtkins: It's a problem with having custom properties in\nL278:                  keyframes that affect animations, currently handled by\nL279:                  tainting.\nL280:       TabAtkins: In ideal non-problematic case, have custom property\nL281:                  defined for element, animation of that property on\nL282:                  keyframes rule\nL283:       TabAtkins: var substitution should reflect the running animation,\nL284:                  not the base value.\nL285:       dbaron: Animations make you compute style.\nL286:       fantasai: How do you animate a string?\nL287:       TabAtkins: flip at 50%\nL288:       * fantasai doesn't think this is useful\nL289:       fantasai: what's the use case for animating custom properties?\nL290:       TabAtkins: Adding ability to type custom properties in Houdini.\nL291:       TabAtkins: Lea also has some examples where it's useful to animate\nL292:                  keywords.\nL293: \nL294:       <birtles> problem with display property:\nL295:                 --foo: block;\nL296:                 animation: yer 1s;\nL297:                 display: var(--foo)\nL298:                 @keyframes yer { from, to { --foo: none } }\nL299: \nL300:       dbaron: How does the global tainting work?\nL301:       TabAtkins: If a custom property is animated in any keyframe\nL302:       TabAtkins: That name is tainted, and becomes an invalid reference\nL303:                  in an animation-* property.\nL304:       dbaron: I think global tainting is less disruptive cuz affects\nL305:               less stuff.\nL306:       shane: Can be bad because blow a way a bunch of rules unexpectedly.\nL307:       dbaron: Can you do the other tainting in both directions?\nL308:       dbaron: Such that if you use a custom property in a keyframe then\nL309:               you taint the value and it can't be used in an animation\nL310:               and... that's harder though.\nL311:       dbaron: To solve frremy's case, need to also taint in other\nL312:               direction,\nL313:       shane: For frremy's case\nL314:       shane: You apply your static style\nL315:       shane: That might trigger some animations\nL316:       shane: And then you apply your animated style after that.\nL317:       shane: Don't update var() references in animation properties.\nL318:       dbaron: As long as we don't send AnimationStart and AnimationEnd\nL319:               events each time.\nL320:       shane: You don't update animation-* properties EVER as a result of\nL321:              animated styles.\nL322:       dbaron: That's actually really hard for us.\nL323:       dbaron: When we apply the animation style, we create a new style\n"
